  "course_description": "Specialized courses in American culture and history. May be repeated for credit when topic varies.",
  "course_notes": "What moves people to write and paint on city walls? How does the presence of art in public space, from small graffiti tags to visceral political slogans and commissioned murals, transform the urban everyday? And what can we learn about a place by reading and analyzing its walls? These are the central questions that animate this critical introduction to graffiti and public art. Engaging with interdisciplinary scholarship alongside visual texts such as documentaries and photographic archives, we will examine the historical lineages, social context, aesthetic techniques, and transformative potential of art in public space. We will trace and discuss the significance of graffiti and public art as tools of expression and intervention for marginalized communities, the interplay between criminalization and institutionalization, and movements for preservation and archiving, amid broader currents of urban and social change. Throughout, Atlanta will serve as the central case study for our engagement. The city¿s rich history and contemporary presence of public art and graffiti will be woven into the curriculum through guest lectures by local artists and researchers, visual archives, and field trips. Many of these encounters and experiences will be activated and deepened through your course assignments.",
